In my main discipline of mathematical physics, in which I seek to model the universe, big and small, I start by identifying a phenomenon that intrigues me. It's always a mishmash of bits and pieces. Hence, I start by trying to sort out all the bits into separate piles. Then, I try to create a mini-model of each bit, then put the mini-models together, to build a bigger model.
I used to do this with a pencil on paper. Now, I do it in my head. Then I walk, run, throw, ride, climb, hop, skip, jump, while I beaver away at reassembling all the bits in my brain. I keep turning over the image of my big model, looking at it from different perspectives, often for many years. Often, I have to pull it apart, start again from scratch. Eventually, I might decide my model is not perfect but works well enough for the time being. I leave it to sit for a while. Later, I return, tinker with it. Leave it. Come back. Repeat. The model is never finished. It's a work in progress.
